#{[17592186315095 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+5,\\, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ -2,\\, & x=1,\\end{cases}\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),\\,h(x)={e}^{1-x}.\n$$"] [17592186315096 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{2}{x+1},\\,g(x)=\\begin{cases}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ -1,\\, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\end{cases}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186315097 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x-1,\\, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2,\\, & x=1,\\end{cases}\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,h(x)={2}^{x+1}.\n$$"] [17592186315098 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,h(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+1,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1,\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186315100 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186315100 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 3.$\n\nFor $s>3,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{5}+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{(3-s)t}}{3-s}\\right)|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(3-s)a}-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{5(3-s)}}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$"] [17592186315101 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186315101 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-s}{[{e}^{(3-s)t}]}_{0}^{2}+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186315102 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186315102 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{(5-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-s}{[{e}^{(5-s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{(5-s)}-1}{5-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186315103 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186315103 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-s}{[{e}^{(1-s)t}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186315105 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-3t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\, & t>1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186315105 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-3t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\, & t>1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(3+s)}{[{e}^{-(3+s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-4{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\frac{4{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186317189 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317194 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317195 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317196 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317223 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317224 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317225 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317227 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317256 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186317271 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+3, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186317343 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186317392 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186317393 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186317467 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186317470 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{0}{1}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317512 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319070 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319085 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319126 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319127 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/83/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319128 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319155 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319156 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(2x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{1/2}^{a} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/2a/2ad762128a32c119f1783b88b4d10ae6820a914689296f03eedad206e097a29d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319158 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319184 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5n-2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\n$$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$ is divergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}\n$$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"] [17592186319185 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-2|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"] [17592186319189 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319192 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}\\right)|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319198 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{3{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=8.\n$$"] [17592186319214 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-8n}{n}+\\frac{7}{n})}{\\frac{4n}{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8+\\frac{7}{n}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-2.$"] [17592186319215 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{5}{n}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}}}{3-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186319216 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{n}}{4+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186319218 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(5{n}^{2}-4)(5{n}^{2}+4)}{{n}^{2}(3-40{n}^{2})}=\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-40{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{25{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{16}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{40{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(25-\\frac{16}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}}-40)} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-0}{0-40} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-\\frac{5}{8}.$"] [17592186319219 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3{n}^{3}-5)(3{n}^{3}+5)}{{n}^{3}(2-27{n}^{3})}=\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-27{n}^{6},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{25}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{3}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{27{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{25}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}-27)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186319220 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}-3)(2{n}^{2}+3)}{{n}^{2}(5-12{n}^{3})}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-12{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{12{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4}{n}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}}-12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0-12} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186319370 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319371 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{y}^{-8/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{5}{y}^{-5/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{5}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{5}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{5}}}) \\\\  & =-3(0-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319372 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{s}^{-7/3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{s}^{-4/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{4}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{4}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{4}}}) \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319373 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{t}^{-13/5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{8}{t}^{-8/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{8}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{a}^{8}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{1}^{8}}}) \\\\  & =-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319386 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}3{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{u}^{-4}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-4}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319387 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319388 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3y+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $y\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{(3y+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{(3y+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{5}{3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{9}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319389 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319390 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320710 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320711 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320712 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(2x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320713 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3y-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $y\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{8}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{32}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320714 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320715 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta -1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $\\theta \\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta -1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{12}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320716 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-3$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{27}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320717 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-12$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-12}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-12)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320741 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320742 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/61/61a2aafc6dadee9e392e098f77839fdd99f3bb5d30c62cf06fef1be08c4b2682.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320744 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-9}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2f/2f4fb9b35e0b68c6155ab9b945d10c36d08d878560e93d34d04e610d1983e50c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{3{e}^{9}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6{e}^{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320745 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](images/ad/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320746 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5a}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-5x}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5x}=0$\n\n![](images/50/5010180e9cd41608abc630cd8974cc69ee78337bcb46a7f079d58eeb8dda9cc2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320747 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x+2}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3a+2}+\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-3x}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=0$\n\n![](images/42/425e55256d2e53db1fce6684e2b7b05e8b487d5107dd4abacb8cf17add287c23.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320748 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a-3}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](images/dd/dd3903454e30d878fee3cd1f58f3942db034fde534e514f99e2c14ddd892a9b3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320803 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320816 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320817 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{a} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320818 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/4d/4daa526482964d826d5cf17a9a646de0a01059e7f886ddd1e19bcb09c4c25ee3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320819 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/fd/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320831 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c2/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320832 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2f/2fc15149d2097a1965e575e725b4dfb49401c1fe5bb1c5b3e43bf0f4f51743a1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320833 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{9\\cdot {3}^{2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/05/052e461c4f02a644254b4b9936b688c29afacd07432db7234bd71474dc9a076c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320853 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186320859 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/8f/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320860 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320862 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/fa/fac2eaeb6dfdda240d2bad7107f7a7e26ff688e89976437a95b99bc3f2ac288f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320863 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/e5/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320864 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/34/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320865 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2a/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320867 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/6a/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320868 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320873 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-3x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](images/99/998ec723fd7212da4382718750465ce313349ed3f2c0258cc1f68e0a5485d968.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320874 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a4961fa6f4f1aafeba2e8f77ed91e7d034fffd6aa249f13bb8357da11a7cea5e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320875 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/24ca4823d220c3eb31127ed28ab7c75b1297e10ae20a1926d112a234806c411e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320876 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]}_{a}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320877 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=2{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320878 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320879 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/c2/c2104caf050dd3bb795f29b40ce3047e7c8cea98f7c0a6abede5f92c23a06815.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{2x}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320880 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/2b/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320892 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}(2x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(2x-6)}{{n}^{2}}| \\\\  & =|2x-6|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =|2x-6|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|2x-6| \\\\  & =2|x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-3|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186320893 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}| \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"]}
